783    /** FUNCTION otap_test.throws_ok
784    * Checks if a given statement or code block throws the expected error message. The expected error message must match
785    * the SQLERRM returned from exception thrown. Use throws_match or throws_like if only parts of the error message must
786    * match. If the statement starts with SELECT it is executed as is, without caring for returned columns or rows and ignoring
787    * any given header definition. The header definition is a code block for the declare section defining variables that may be
788    * needed by called functions or procedures. It is only used if the statement does not start with SELECT and if the declaration
789    * block can be executed with a NULL procedure without throwing exceptions.
790    *
791    * Function comes in two flavors, comparing SQLERRM as VARCHAR2 or the error code SQLCODE as number. It can also be used to check
792    * if a statement does not cause any exception by switching the expected result to otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_FAILED.
793    *
794    * SQL statements (SELECT, UPDATE, DELETE) MUST NOT have a trailing semicolon. otap will try to detect and remove it, but may fail.
795    * Semicolon in dynamically executed SQL statements will cause an unexpected exception.
796    *
797    * Functions and procedures MUST have a trailing semicolon, especially if more than one command is executed in the block.
798    *
799    * Syntax errors will most likely cause a different exception as the expected one and fail the test. This is especially important if
800    * you switch the expected test result. otap expects in this case, that the statement could be executed without any exception.
801    *
802    * @param p_statement Mandatory. The statement as string to execute. Can be a select statement or function/procedure call. No need to proovide a begin end block. Syntax should be checked or will cause unexpected exceptions.
803    * @param p_sqlerrm Mandatory. The exact case sensitive expected error message as returned by SQLERRM after a provoked exception.
804    * @param p_header_def Optional valid header definition (test result is undefined if header is not valid) for function or procedure tests to support OUT and return variables.
805    * @param p_description The test description if any. If not given, a description is generated, see template.
806    * @param p_expected_result The expected test result as number. Default is test passed. See otap_constants.
807    * @param p_schema A schema override of the current test session if needed, taken as is. Compares are not clearly associated to a schema. Case sensitive.
808    *
809    * @return The test result as text.
810    *
811    * Examples (english messages)
812    * SELECT otap_test.throws_ok('SELECT 1/0 FROM dual', 'ORA-01476: divisor is equal to zero') AS test_result FROM dual;
813    * SELECT otap_test.throws_ok('l_return := 1/0;', 'ORA-01476: divisor is equal to zero', 'l_return NUMBER;') AS test_result FROM dual;
814    */
815    FUNCTION throws_ok( p_statement	     IN VARCHAR2
816  			 , p_sqlerrm	     IN VARCHAR2
817  			 , p_header_def      IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
818  			 , p_description     IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
819  			 , p_expected_result IN NUMBER	 DEFAULT otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_PASSED
820  			 , p_schema	     IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT SYS_CONTEXT('USERENV', 'CURRENT_SCHEMA')
821  			 )
822  	 RETURN VARCHAR2
823    ;
824  
825    /** FUNCTION otap_test.throws_ok
826    * Flavor error code SQLCODE as number. Same behavior as flavor error message. Be aware that some Oracle error codes are group error codes where
827    * the error message differs depending on the exact error cause.
828    *
829    * @param p_statement Mandatory. The statement as string to execute. Can be a select statement or function/procedure call. No need to proovide a begin end block. Syntax should be checked or will cause unexpected exceptions.
830    * @param p_sqlcode Mandatory. The exact expected error code as returned by SQLCODE after a provoked exception.
831    * @param p_header_def Optional valid header definition (test result is undefined if header is not valid) for function or procedure tests to support OUT and return variables.
832    * @param p_description The test description if any. If not given, a description is generated, see template.
833    * @param p_expected_result The expected test result as number. Default is test passed. See otap_constants.
834    * @param p_schema A schema override of the current test session if needed, taken as is. Compares are not clearly associated to a schema. Case sensitive.
835    *
836    * @return The test result as text.
837    *
838    * Example
839    * SELECT otap_test.throws_ok('SELECT 1/0 FROM dual', -1476) AS test_result FROM dual;
840    */
841    FUNCTION throws_ok( p_statement	     IN VARCHAR2
842  			 , p_sqlcode	     IN NUMBER
843  			 , p_header_def      IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
844  			 , p_description     IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
845  			 , p_expected_result IN NUMBER	 DEFAULT otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_PASSED
846  			 , p_schema	     IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT SYS_CONTEXT('USERENV', 'CURRENT_SCHEMA')
847  			 )
848  	 RETURN VARCHAR2
849    ;
850  
851    /** FUNCTION otap_test.throws_matches
852    * Same as otap_test.throws_ok apart from using Oracle REGEXP to identify the error message.
853    *
854    * @param p_statement Mandatory. The statement as string to execute. Can be a select statement or function/procedure call. No need to proovide a begin end block. Syntax should be checked or will cause unexpected exceptions.
855    * @param p_regex_sqlerrm Mandatory. The regular expression to match the expected error message as returned by SQLERRM after a provoked exception.
856    * @param p_param Optional valid parameter for REGEXP_LIKE. 'i' means case insensitive. Parameters are case sensitive. See Oracle documentation for details, https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/21/sqlrf/Pattern-matching-Conditions.html#GUID-D2124F3A-C6E4-4CCA-A40E-2FFCABFD8E19.
857    * @param p_header_def Optional valid header definition (test result is undefined if header is not valid) for function or procedure tests to support OUT and return variables.
858    * @param p_description The test description if any. If not given, a description is generated, see template.
859    * @param p_expected_result The expected test result as number. Default is test passed. See otap_constants.
860    * @param p_schema A schema override of the current test session if needed, taken as is. Compares are not clearly associated to a schema. Case sensitive.
861    *
862    * @return The test result as text.
863    *
864    * Example (english message)
865    * SELECT otap_test.throws_matches('SELECT 1/0 FROM dual', '.*-01476: divisor is equal to zero.*') FROM dual;
866    */
867    FUNCTION throws_matches( p_statement	  IN VARCHAR2
868  			      , p_regex_sqlerrm   IN VARCHAR2
869  			      , p_param 	  IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
870  			      , p_header_def	  IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
871  			      , p_description	  IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
872  			      , p_expected_result IN NUMBER   DEFAULT otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_PASSED
873  			      , p_schema	  IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT SYS_CONTEXT('USERENV', 'CURRENT_SCHEMA')
874  			      )
875  	 RETURN VARCHAR2
876    ;
877  
878    /** FUNCTION otap_test.throws_like
879    * Same as otap_test.throws_ok apart from using Oracle LIKE to identify the error message.
880    *
881    * @param p_statement Mandatory. The statement as string to execute. Can be a select statement or function/procedure call. No need to proovide a begin end block. Syntax should be checked or will cause unexpected exceptions.
882    * @param p_like_sqlerrm Mandatory. The LIKE expression to match the expected error message as returned by SQLERRM after a provoked exception.
883    * @param p_case_sensitive Optional defines that the compared result is handled as case sensitive, if set to otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TRUE.
